WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.580
Mission: Incredible or Mission:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:07.399
Unbearable? Here's the best and worst of&nbsp;
Tom Cruise's greatest action franchise.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:12.600
First of all, it's worth pointing out that&nbsp;
being the "worst" in an indisputably phenomenal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:17.940
franchise isn't such a bad thing. It's not&nbsp;
that director John Woo's submission to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:23.340
IMF compendium is bad, per se, it's just that it&nbsp;
doesn't hold a candle to the other five films.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:28.260
The movie pairs Tom Cruise's Ethan Hunt with&nbsp;
professional thief Nyah Hall as they battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:33.420
her ex-boyfriend — who also happens to be a rogue&nbsp;
IMF agent. The baddie in this one is attempting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:38.280
to steal a deadly virus and start a worldwide&nbsp;
pandemic — a plotline that has arguably aged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:42.660
better than anyone could have guessed at the time.&nbsp;
Either way, Mission: Impossible II is not the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:47.339
strongest of the series, and it would be six years&nbsp;
before Ethan chose to accept his next mission.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:53.400
Yes, the stunt on the Burj Khalifa is cool.&nbsp;
Nobody's denying that. But that scene doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.140
make up for Paula Patton's painful turn as&nbsp;
Jane, or Jeremy Renner's unbearably whiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:03.000
Brandt. If it wasn't for his wisecracking buddy&nbsp;
Benji, Ethan might have thought about scrapping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.040
the entire team and starting fresh after Ghost&nbsp;
Protocol. But in fairness, director Brad Bird's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:12.180
franchise entry does an excellent job of setting&nbsp;
up the so-called "dashies;" the most recent three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:16.800
films in the franchise, which take a decidedly&nbsp;
cooler approach than the three that preceded them.

00:01:16.926 --> 00:01:20.106
"Mission accomplished!"

00:01:20.106 --> 00:01:23.580
The major set pieces lose a little urgency&nbsp;
when you're watching them on a small screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.220
but all is forgiven for the&nbsp;
movie's most thrilling moments:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:30.840
Benji inciting a Russian prison riot against&nbsp;
his better judgment, for example, or he and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:36.060
Ethan using virtual reality trickery to break into&nbsp;
the Kremlin. That's the IMF we all know and love.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.900
There's nothing quite like&nbsp;
an original — or, at least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.860
an adaptation of the real original. If nothing&nbsp;
else, though, the existence of the Mission:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:48.960
Impossible TV series just goes to show that the&nbsp;
party never starts until Tom Cruise shows up.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:54.180
It's hard to overstate the impact of Mission:&nbsp;
Impossible when it debuted in 1996. This was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.440
movie that killed off half the cast in the first&nbsp;
half hour, delivered a stealth scene so iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:03.300
it would be parodied for years to come, and, of&nbsp;
course, introduced a new generation to an iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:07.740
theme song. Perhaps a good example of how the&nbsp;
first of the franchise shook the status quo is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:12.840
the fact that audiences were originally baffled —&nbsp;
some even angry — at how complicated and confusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:17.520
the plot was. If you rewatch it today, however,&nbsp;
you'll experience a storyline that feels as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.530
elegant as it is easy to follow.

00:02:20.724 --> 00:02:24.660
The top three Mission: Impossible movies&nbsp;
are a little trickier to parse, because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:28.740
honestly, they're all great. Mission:&nbsp;
Impossible III, by director J.J. Abrams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.800
marked the end of one era and the&nbsp;
beginning of another; every Mission:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.340
Impossible is bigger and bolder than the&nbsp;
last, but the films that follow this one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:39.300
scale up considerably in terms of&nbsp;
stunts, scope, and storytelling.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:44.100
Mission: Impossible III sets up that change just&nbsp;
right, introducing us to Ethan Hunt's foray into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:48.540
domestic life and bringing back crowd favorite&nbsp;
Luther, establishing that, for Ethan Hunt, people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:53.460
matter more than politics. As such, the film also&nbsp;
kicks off a question Hunt will be attempting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:57.780
answer for the next three flicks: How can he&nbsp;
save the world and the people he cares about?

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:01.080
The movie was the lowest-grossing of&nbsp;
the franchise, and reviews were mixed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.600
but standout performances&nbsp;
by Keri Russell and Philip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.983
Seymour Hoffman suggest it's a lot&nbsp;
better than it was given credit for.

00:03:06.983 --> 00:03:12.420
"Oh, that was nothing. That was… fun."

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:17.400
Moreover, it's a joy to see all the emotional&nbsp;
themes that were established in this movie and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:21.120
carried on by the rest: Ethan Hunt as&nbsp;
a mentor, Ethan Hunt as a man in love,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:26.100
Ethan Hunt as a team player and an all-around&nbsp;
good guy. All in all, a retrospective viewing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:31.020
gives the third installment a shine that critics&nbsp;
might not have seen when it premiered in 2006.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:35.160
Mission: Impossible — Fallout&nbsp;
is the most recent film of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.160
franchise, and it picks up where its&nbsp;
predecessor, Rogue Nation, left off.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:42.240
Ethan Hunt and the gang are still hot on the&nbsp;
heels of the Syndicate — a shadowy organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:46.740
composed of former field operatives who have&nbsp;
since become mercenaries. A new version of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:51.120
Syndicate called the Apostles has wacky ideas&nbsp;
about plutonium, dirty bombs, and suffering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.140
leading to a new world order, among other things.&nbsp;
None of this really matters, though, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:59.520
if you're watching Fallout, you're probably on&nbsp;
board with whatever the IMF is up to these days.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.560
Many favorite faces return in Fallout, but new&nbsp;
additions to the cast, such as Henry Cavill and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:09.060
Angela Bassett, make the film even more fun. The&nbsp;
movie feels like a genuine rollercoaster ride,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:13.260
with non-stop action, breathless ups and downs,&nbsp;
and a few loop-de-loops thrown in for good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:17.760
measure. It's this movie that makes you realize&nbsp;
that, no matter how far-fetched or implausible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:22.061
these flicks might get, you're always in good&nbsp;
hands with Christopher McQuarrie and Tom Cruise.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:27.000
Picking a favorite in the Mission: Impossible&nbsp;
series is difficult, but not altogether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.560
impossible. Case in point: Rogue Nation.
Not only does this movie introduce Alec&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:35.760
Baldwin and Sean Harris, the latter of whom&nbsp;
is arguably the franchise's best villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.000
but Christopher McQuarrie takes the&nbsp;
whole series from good to great.

00:04:39.281 --> 00:04:46.481
"Gentleman, this is Solomon&nbsp;
Lane. Mr. Lane… meet the IMF."

00:04:46.481 --> 00:04:49.980
McQuarrie knows you can't just stack&nbsp;
cool fight scenes on top of one another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:54.120
so he uses clever jokes, stunning visuals, and&nbsp;
well-written characters to create a house of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:58.320
cards so perfect you'll be holding your breath&nbsp;
and praying it doesn't all come crashing down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:03.300
Tom Cruise cements the movie, of course, with the&nbsp;
same charisma that won hearts in the first film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:07.020
but what knocks Rogue Nation into&nbsp;
the top spot is Rebecca Ferguson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:10.680
who makes her franchise debut with&nbsp;
the character of Ilsa Faust. Faust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:14.340
is more than a match for Hunt, and&nbsp;
she steals every scene she's in.

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:18.900
Throw it all together and you've got the best&nbsp;
Mission: Impossible movie — though, with McQuarrie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:22.860
and Cruise set to return for Dead Reckoning,&nbsp;
this one may not be in the top spot for long.

